WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.446
Hey! Welcome to new Project Kamp update!

00:00:02.446 --> 00:00:03.603
This is&nbsp;Javier!

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.224
I'm from the video maker team.

00:00:05.224 --> 00:00:09.077
Normally, I will be behind the camera,
but this time I'm&nbsp;going to be in front.

00:00:09.077 --> 00:00:12.048
And, I'm going to present you this video, which is the challenges video.

00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:16.386
So, every&nbsp;ten videos, we do a challenge one,
 which are the ones that end with an 8.

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:20.220
And, we talk about our previous,&nbsp;current and coming challenges.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.579
Also, we do a Q&amp;A!

00:00:22.579 --> 00:00:25.915
So, well, we're going to start now.

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:29.960
First, we&nbsp;going to Julie with our previous challenges.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.779
- Okay, let me tell you something 
about the&nbsp;previous challenges we had.

00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:38.957
At the moment, the summer peak is gone.

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:40.307
So, quite some people&nbsp;left already.

00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:42.488
For instance, the managing of the teams.

00:00:42.853 --> 00:00:44.402
This becomes much easier!

00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:50.100
Probably because&nbsp;the teams are getting way smaller,
 so there are less things going on at the same time.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.944
But&nbsp;also, the people in the teams, 
they are here for quite a long time already.

00:00:53.944 --> 00:00:57.338
So, they know how&nbsp;we work 
and this makes everything go way smoother.

00:00:57.338 --> 00:01:00.635
Among the people that have left 
were Wouter and&nbsp;Debbie with their baby.

00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:05.126
This was quite a challenge we took on this year,
 with having a baby on the Kamp.

00:01:05.126 --> 00:01:07.084
But, overall, it went quite well!

00:01:07.084 --> 00:01:09.919
A lot of small challenges that we have learned a lot from.

00:01:09.919 --> 00:01:11.742
But, we also&nbsp;enjoyed many things!

00:01:11.742 --> 00:01:13.618
Another thing: it's way less hot.

00:01:13.618 --> 00:01:17.300
It might not look like it,
but I'm&nbsp;wearing long pants with socks and sandals.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.337
And, actually, this relates to a lot of challenges we had.

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:21.690
For example: the working schedule.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.384
This can be just staying as it was,
 because we can work&nbsp;during the whole day.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:26.485
It's not extremely hot.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:28.963
So, also, we can use the power tools whenever we need&nbsp;to.

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:34.129
This helps a lot, and it saves us some more energy
 by the end of the day to do other stuff.

00:01:34.129 --> 00:01:37.677
Another nice thing about the summer ending
 is that there is way less fire risk.

00:01:37.677 --> 00:01:41.237
Actually, we had&nbsp;already some rain, which is really helpful!

00:01:41.237 --> 00:01:43.397
But, this doesn't mean that we can chill...

00:01:43.397 --> 00:01:46.944
The fire in&nbsp;2017 was around autumn time.

00:01:46.944 --> 00:01:52.867
So, we keep on taking precautions, care of the native trees
 and making&nbsp;the land more fire resilient bit by bit.

00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:55.262
We have all of these challenges quite under control!

00:01:55.262 --> 00:01:57.817
But, we also have a long lasting challenge.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.992
Actually, one that we haven't updated you for a while!

00:01:59.992 --> 00:02:02.608
But, Dave is going to tell you more about that one.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.607
- All right, so, the Community Center...

00:02:12.229 --> 00:02:13.553
We have a few&nbsp;big challenges here.

00:02:13.553 --> 00:02:17.163
You guys have also been asking for 
what the status is on this project.

00:02:17.163 --> 00:02:19.807
So, let me give you a good overview.

00:02:20.871 --> 00:02:23.237
Originally, we always thought this would become a workspace.

00:02:23.663 --> 00:02:27.974
But, later on, by being here, we realized what is 
more important, which is a Community Center.

00:02:27.974 --> 00:02:32.816
And, it's&nbsp;quite an important building, because it's going 
to decide how many people we can host on the&nbsp;land.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:36.658
When we saw the size of the building, we took some measurements.

00:02:36.658 --> 00:02:38.641
And, we started figuring out on what we want in there.

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:40.729
We figured: ''Let's design it for 50 people.''

00:02:40.729 --> 00:02:44.041
Which means we need to have a kitchen&nbsp;that can host 50 people.

00:02:44.041 --> 00:02:45.700
Maybe somewhere there?

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.914
We need to have toilets and showers...

00:02:47.914 --> 00:02:49.544
Maybe somewhere&nbsp;over there?

00:02:49.544 --> 00:02:55.054
A big living room, when it's raining outside or too hot
 that we can be there also in a nice&nbsp;place.

00:02:55.054 --> 00:02:59.314
And, some offices for 10 people that could perhaps be upstairs.

00:03:00.696 --> 00:03:04.146
This sort of gave us&nbsp;some requirements of 
what we want out of this building.

00:03:04.146 --> 00:03:09.338
The next step was trying to figure&nbsp;out what would it actually look like.

00:03:09.338 --> 00:03:11.969
So we started on playing a bit with the design.

00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:13.488
Let me show you&nbsp;what we've done!

00:03:13.488 --> 00:03:17.096
The first thing was making a 3D model, 
out of the existing structure.

00:03:18.347 --> 00:03:20.270
It's&nbsp;for us to understand better on how big it is.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:23.074
But, we also wanted to invite the online community to&nbsp;help out.

00:03:23.074 --> 00:03:27.077
So, we've put it online, so people could submit their designs,
with the requirements we&nbsp;had.

00:03:27.825 --> 00:03:29.009
We have a bunch of different ones.

00:03:29.009 --> 00:03:31.417
Some people decided to use two different buildings...

00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:33.611
Someone made one big building...

00:03:33.611 --> 00:03:35.620
Someone made the double floor.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.014
So, we have a bunch of designs coming in!

00:03:38.014 --> 00:03:43.884
And, in the meanwhile, we also started working with the floor plan, like:
''How do we actually arrange the&nbsp;space?''

00:03:43.884 --> 00:03:45.683
''Where does it make sense to have a kitchen?''

00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:47.333
''Where does the sun come up?'' 

00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:49.008
''What's it like in&nbsp;the winter and summer?''

00:03:49.342 --> 00:03:54.629
We're really trying to position all the elements properly in this place.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.750
One&nbsp;guy, who is helping out, 
is Adrian from France, has been very helpful!

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.397
He was really excited, so&nbsp;we continued making 
more drawings with him as well.

00:04:03.397 --> 00:04:05.254
We were going more detailed really!

00:04:06.197 --> 00:04:09.360
But, we also&nbsp;noticed, like, we make all these drawings...

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.826
We kind of also need to make sure what is legally allowed!

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:18.550
Are there any rules and requirements from the outside world
 towards this building&nbsp;in terms of fire regulations?

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.652
Or, maybe, some types of materials we can use?

00:04:21.078 --> 00:04:26.167
So, before we go too deep&nbsp;into the design,
 we first needed to get into some paperwork.

00:04:31.261 --> 00:04:33.143
All right, so, we're currently in our&nbsp;local town!

00:04:33.143 --> 00:04:37.913
In this building is the architect of the municipality,
 that keeps an overview of&nbsp;all the rules and regulations.

00:04:37.913 --> 00:04:42.860
So, we went there a bunch of times with our paperwork,
 to see what's&nbsp;allowed and what isn't.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:48.788
The main talks we had with the municipality was, on the one hand,
that we need&nbsp;to legalize the existing structure.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:52.237
Because, the previous owner just built a building there,
 but&nbsp;it was never in the paperwork...

00:04:52.237 --> 00:04:53.223
So, we need to put it in.

00:04:53.223 --> 00:04:56.861
And then, you need to look at the maps, 
fire&nbsp;regulations and all this kind of stuff.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.768
Then, we also wanted to know if we want 
to build a new&nbsp;building and on what is allowed.

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:04.886
Which depends a bit on you what are you going to do...

00:05:04.886 --> 00:05:05.961
Are you going to sell&nbsp;stuff?

00:05:05.961 --> 00:05:08.067
Are you going to produce things?

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:11.458
Is it for people from outside or just you internally?

00:05:11.458 --> 00:05:16.180
You&nbsp;need to find out what's the 
right category to build your building.

00:05:17.001 --> 00:05:20.698
So, this means it's just a lot of&nbsp;back and forth 
with the municipality, to see what's allowed.

00:05:20.698 --> 00:05:26.627
And, I think, also during this process,
 we&nbsp;realized you need to get quite specific.

00:05:26.627 --> 00:05:29.245
Because, every little decision has an effect&nbsp;somewhere!

00:05:29.823 --> 00:05:35.936
So, it's a good reality check to see 
that it's easy to dream big with these buildings...

00:05:35.936 --> 00:05:40.068
But then, this sort of really funnels it down
 to what is allowed in this setting.

00:05:40.068 --> 00:05:43.871
Just on what we&nbsp;can do what type of building we're going to build.

00:05:43.871 --> 00:05:45.795
So, we got a lot of good information!

00:05:45.795 --> 00:05:46.869
A lot&nbsp;of rules and guidelines!

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:50.602
And now, we can go a bit deeper into the design process.

00:05:51.514 --> 00:05:52.823
What's&nbsp;the building going to look like?

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:58.000
So, it's time to look into some of the designs.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.024
The fun part!

00:05:59.541 --> 00:06:01.516
Let me show&nbsp;you some of the things that were made!

00:06:01.516 --> 00:06:04.348
So, the original plan was that this is the building.

00:06:04.348 --> 00:06:06.745
We use as much existing structures as possible.

00:06:07.201 --> 00:06:11.560
We build a big roof on top and we fill up the holes
 with other materials,&nbsp;like glass.

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:14.308
So, from the back, it would look like this.

00:06:14.308 --> 00:06:18.333
You can see the bricks and then the holes 
with&nbsp;some other wooden frames inside.

00:06:19.124 --> 00:06:22.398
So, from the top, the building would have one big roof.

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:27.379
And, if we&nbsp;open it up, we keep the existing 
inner walls to arrange the space.

00:06:27.379 --> 00:06:30.066
To reuse as much possible.

00:06:30.066 --> 00:06:33.799
So, from the top, you would have a kitchen area here.

00:06:34.103 --> 00:06:36.544
More toilets and the sink here.

00:06:36.544 --> 00:06:38.755
And, the&nbsp;eating area here.

00:06:40.245 --> 00:06:42.596
So, here's a little clip, where you can see the inside.

00:06:44.333 --> 00:06:49.566
As you can see, the space has&nbsp;a lot of beams and reinforcements.

00:06:49.566 --> 00:06:53.170
Just because the bricks are super fragile and brittle.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:54.680
Also&nbsp;because the building burned down!

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.619
So, to provide structural strength, we need to add that.

00:06:59.138 --> 00:07:06.658
But, while also talking to the municipality, we realized it's going 
to be quite hard to actually legalize&nbsp;this existing structure.

00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:12.417
Because, it's very unknown, based on the strength 
and that it's not very properly built...

00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:16.220
And also, if you want the building to be replicable for others...

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:19.062
It's kind of weird to&nbsp;start off with this very specific structure.

00:07:19.062 --> 00:07:25.777
So, we slowly gave up on the idea of having 
to use&nbsp;the existing structure.

00:07:26.476 --> 00:07:30.702
We started thinking more like: 
''Okay, what if we build it from scratch?'' 

00:07:30.702 --> 00:07:34.945
So, we&nbsp;keep the existing footprint of the building 
and what it would look like from the outside.

00:07:34.945 --> 00:07:41.086
But, we&nbsp;change the inside, so this gives freedom 
to rearrange of the building itself a bit.

00:07:41.086 --> 00:07:45.705
Yeah, so, this&nbsp;is one version based on that.

00:07:45.705 --> 00:07:50.098
But, we also decided at some point to look 
into keeping&nbsp;the sides more open.

00:07:50.098 --> 00:07:52.702
With an insulated space in the middle.

00:07:52.702 --> 00:07:57.773
We moved it a bit more towards the&nbsp;left,
 so we can take advantage of the morning sun here.

00:07:57.773 --> 00:08:02.245
So, we start started looking more into those&nbsp;areas as well, like:

00:08:02.245 --> 00:08:04.310
''What is the ventilation in the building like?''

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:05.805
''How is the air flow?''

00:08:05.805 --> 00:08:10.409
And because, for&nbsp;instance here on the back side,
 you have the wall into the ground.

00:08:10.409 --> 00:08:11.850
So, it gives a lot of cooling.

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.086
But,&nbsp;also things like the sun:

00:08:14.086 --> 00:08:17.053
''How do we take advantage of 
the winter sun to heat up the building?''

00:08:17.053 --> 00:08:18.925
''But, not&nbsp;from the summer?''

00:08:18.925 --> 00:08:20.907
Because then, it heats up too much...

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.373
With all of that, we started making&nbsp;our final layout,
on what we think would be good for the building.

00:08:29.373 --> 00:08:33.861
So, what we have now is a&nbsp;big kitchen here and a dining room here.

00:08:33.861 --> 00:08:40.107
A living room here, and a technical space 
where we can have the&nbsp;solar and the water system.

00:08:40.533 --> 00:08:44.466
And here, like a cellar for the dry food for the kitchen.

00:08:44.466 --> 00:08:47.596
Here a wet&nbsp;cellar, which is like this underground fridge.

00:08:47.596 --> 00:08:50.403
Because it's underground here, it naturally cools&nbsp;it.

00:08:50.403 --> 00:08:53.484
And then, a stairs to the top floor.

00:08:54.123 --> 00:08:57.407
On the top floor, you would have it open here.

00:08:57.407 --> 00:08:59.933
So, you&nbsp;can look down to the eating and the living area.

00:08:59.933 --> 00:09:03.083
Here's an office, some showers and toilets...

00:09:04.147 --> 00:09:06.058
So, we also&nbsp;made a few visuals!

00:09:06.058 --> 00:09:09.455
It was mainly for the municipality 
to get them excited about this building.

00:09:10.364 --> 00:09:12.885
This&nbsp;is what it looks like from the front.

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:17.128
We had to lower the height a bit actually,
 when showing&nbsp;the plan throughout time to the municipality.

00:09:17.128 --> 00:09:20.180
Because, we would exceed it's height...

00:09:21.859 --> 00:09:24.358
Here&nbsp;you can see that it's built under a slope.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.745
On the backside, you have the entrance as well.

00:09:28.745 --> 00:09:32.437
So, you can&nbsp;exit on the first floor from the inside,
 or here from the back.

00:09:33.679 --> 00:09:37.887
From the front, you would also have an&nbsp;outside area,
 where you can eat more in the summer.

00:09:37.887 --> 00:09:39.967
You can eat nicely outside.

00:09:39.967 --> 00:09:43.614
And then, on the inside, we&nbsp;could use some
 Precious Plastic bricks to make the walls.

00:09:44.114 --> 00:09:45.977
Here, you can see the stairs going to the&nbsp;top.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.836
So, this is the inside, and we're pretty happy with the design now!

00:09:49.836 --> 00:09:51.347
It has everything we need!

00:09:51.347 --> 00:09:54.355
So,&nbsp;here you have a big kitchen,
 where we can cook for 50 people.

00:09:54.355 --> 00:09:57.221
Here, they can all eat and have enough space&nbsp;for that.

00:09:57.221 --> 00:10:04.332
In the back, a living room and then on the top,
 you have the offices the showers and&nbsp;the toilets.

00:10:04.332 --> 00:10:08.927
So, this checks all our boxes and fits our needs, which is cool!

00:10:08.927 --> 00:10:10.852
We're happy&nbsp;with it!

00:10:10.852 --> 00:10:12.492
Now, we need to move to the next step.

00:10:12.492 --> 00:10:17.551
All right, so, lastly, let's get into the&nbsp;
current challenges we have in this whole process.

00:10:20.049 --> 00:10:22.455
The first one: ''What kind of material we going to&nbsp;use?''

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:28.320
Because, we decided to break it down,
 due to it not being a 'very easy to legalize' structure,&nbsp;like this.

00:10:29.634 --> 00:10:32.585
So, we're looking into what kind of natural materials can we use.

00:10:32.585 --> 00:10:35.751
We have more an&nbsp;option of using something traditional.

00:10:35.751 --> 00:10:38.970
Like, straw bale mixed with clay.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:43.745
You also have more&nbsp;newer kind of materials, like hempcrete.

00:10:43.745 --> 00:10:49.112
Or, using really an old school way of building,
 which is&nbsp;more traditional as well.

00:10:49.112 --> 00:10:53.272
It's with big granite rocks which is a 
very local material&nbsp;you'll find a lot around here.

00:10:53.272 --> 00:10:56.824
But, we kind of need to decide which material to use...

00:10:57.258 --> 00:11:00.924
It has a&nbsp;big impact in terms of budgeting and finding the material.

00:11:00.924 --> 00:11:02.945
Also, finding the right people that can work&nbsp;with it.

00:11:03.819 --> 00:11:04.908
The main challenge is just this:

00:11:04.908 --> 00:11:08.985
''How can we make sure it's going to last
in the humid&nbsp;periods in the winter?''

00:11:08.985 --> 00:11:13.342
''And, also how can it maintain a good temperature
 in the super hot summers?''

00:11:13.342 --> 00:11:18.232
So, we're trying to figure out and decide 
on what is the best material to use.

00:11:19.185 --> 00:11:22.224
The second challenge is&nbsp;about the utilities.

00:11:23.026 --> 00:11:24.793
So, to really calculate how much do we need.

00:11:24.793 --> 00:11:28.519
If we know we're going to be with&nbsp;50 people,
what's the size of the solar system we need for instance?

00:11:28.519 --> 00:11:33.675
To make sure we power&nbsp;everything,
so the batteries and the wattage we need to generate.

00:11:33.675 --> 00:11:35.180
Also, the water system.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.565
What's&nbsp;the capacity we need?

00:11:36.565 --> 00:11:38.940
How big of a storage tank we need for the buffer?

00:11:39.396 --> 00:11:40.620
The sewage system...

00:11:41.802 --> 00:11:43.918
Also, the&nbsp;utilities in the kitchen itself.

00:11:43.918 --> 00:11:45.588
Like, what kind of equipment do we need?

00:11:45.588 --> 00:11:47.722
How much and which kind of&nbsp;devices?

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:50.925
Yeah, this is something we really need to nail down...

00:11:50.925 --> 00:11:53.944
Because, it would also affect the building a bit 
and the arrangement inside.

00:11:53.944 --> 00:11:56.769
So, it's an important question we need to answer!

00:11:56.769 --> 00:11:58.866
The&nbsp;third challenge is finances.

00:11:58.866 --> 00:12:02.069
And, just so you know, all these challenges are in a research module.

00:12:02.069 --> 00:12:03.709
So, you can follow the whole process!

00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:07.628
And, you can also give comments and suggestions,
 if you have, any which is very helpful.

00:12:07.628 --> 00:12:11.788
It's better to put them there, than in the YouTube comments, because&nbsp;it's a bit messy in YouTube...

00:12:11.788 --> 00:12:13.579
Anyway, the finances!

00:12:13.579 --> 00:12:18.959
So, we roughly measured that the building would&nbsp;be 
between €200.000 and €300.000 to completely build.

00:12:19.658 --> 00:12:21.766
To have all the utilities inside!

00:12:21.766 --> 00:12:25.779
which is a big&nbsp;upgrade for the project
 because we can host much more people.

00:12:25.992 --> 00:12:28.989
But, it's also quite a big chunk of&nbsp;money that we don't have...

00:12:30.219 --> 00:12:34.311
And, we're in this weird position a bit,
 because officially if you file&nbsp;for your paperwork...

00:12:34.311 --> 00:12:37.535
You need to build the building within 2 years after that.

00:12:37.535 --> 00:12:40.946
But, we don't know if&nbsp;we have the money,
 so we don't know if you can commit to that.

00:12:40.946 --> 00:12:46.318
Or, you do it the other way around, where you say you first 
get the money and then you have the final drawing.

00:12:46.318 --> 00:12:49.068
So, we're not sure&nbsp;what's the best approach here to take.

00:12:49.068 --> 00:12:53.785
In terms of the money, we were thinking of doing&nbsp;a crowd fund.

00:12:53.785 --> 00:12:56.349
We're not really sure yet how or how to set this up...

00:12:56.349 --> 00:12:59.963
But, in general, we really like you&nbsp;guys for all of the support!

00:12:59.963 --> 00:13:02.965
We also know it's a lot of money to fund from people.

00:13:02.965 --> 00:13:04.339
So, we're not sure if&nbsp;we're going to make it.

00:13:04.947 --> 00:13:07.859
I think it's also one of the things we always have in mind.

00:13:07.859 --> 00:13:12.378
That, in one hand,&nbsp;the building would help
 to be with more people on the land.

00:13:12.378 --> 00:13:13.842
Which then can do more useful research...

00:13:13.842 --> 00:13:19.446
But, also,&nbsp;we want the building to be replicable,
 so others around the world that want to start a community...

00:13:19.446 --> 00:13:21.207
They can use the drawing as a base.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.759
So, it should be somewhat useful, because drawings like&nbsp;this...

00:13:24.759 --> 00:13:25.887
You don't really find them.

00:13:25.887 --> 00:13:31.454
You maybe find a drawing for a house or like a small household,
 but not&nbsp;a Community Center for multiple people.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.180
So, in a way, we also feel the drawing is useful for the world.

00:13:35.758 --> 00:13:38.429
But, anyway, whilst thinking about this, we also realized:

00:13:38.429 --> 00:13:46.848
''How do you get this amount of&nbsp;money for a project, 
that is somewhat for the world, but it might be exclusive for here?''

00:13:47.091 --> 00:13:50.000
And, I guess&nbsp;we realized we don't really have our vision very clear...

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.965
What does this project really want to do?

00:13:51.965 --> 00:13:53.562
What's the plan in the long term?

00:13:53.927 --> 00:13:56.680
So, then you also know if it's worth to support it.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:14:00.359
But, this is&nbsp;another challenge that Rita
 is going to talk a bit more about!

00:14:00.359 --> 00:14:02.226
- Yes, the vision.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:08.946
So, a few years ago, we arrived&nbsp;to the land with a notion of 
wanting to live a more sustainable way.

00:14:09.432 --> 00:14:11.714
But, we didn't really have a&nbsp;plan for that...

00:14:11.714 --> 00:14:17.320
And, it was really good, because it gave us one to two years
 to just observe and to get to&nbsp;know the land.

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:20.339
But now, as the project complexifies and grows...

00:14:20.339 --> 00:14:24.283
We feel the need to create really&nbsp;a vision
 that aligns the team a bit more.

00:14:24.283 --> 00:14:28.905
Just so that it's easier to communicate with you guys,
 our audience,&nbsp;or to anyone that likes the project.

00:14:28.905 --> 00:14:35.710
The complexity in that is that the project is quite broad
 and it&nbsp;plays with words like 'sustainability'.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:39.393
There is a different interpretation for each person that&nbsp;hears it.

00:14:40.731 --> 00:14:44.563
We really need to go into that deeper,
 and to understand that deeper.

00:14:44.563 --> 00:14:48.828
Because,&nbsp;it will also help us to do the long-term documentation.

00:14:48.828 --> 00:14:51.051
So far, we have been doing these weekly recaps.

00:14:51.051 --> 00:14:54.921
It's super cool, entertaining and it help us to document a bit.

00:14:54.921 --> 00:14:57.151
But, it doesn't go very deep in&nbsp;knowledge...

00:14:57.151 --> 00:15:03.245
What we aim on is to really share a bundle
 of information that can be replicatable.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:08.176
The challenge in there is that we live in 
a very specific context and different setting.

00:15:08.176 --> 00:15:12.780
So, it has&nbsp;very specific needs and very specific policies.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.812
And, we want to find out what kind of information can be&nbsp;useful.

00:15:15.812 --> 00:15:20.107
For example, to live in the jungle on the outskirts of a city.

00:15:21.437 --> 00:15:27.545
This is basically just&nbsp;going through a big pile of information,
 sorting what's useful and what's not useful.

00:15:27.545 --> 00:15:32.482
And, on the other&nbsp;hand, we also have a day-to-day stuff
 that takes over all of our capacity...

00:15:32.482 --> 00:15:37.416
But, as the system grows&nbsp;stronger and more self-sufficient...

00:15:37.416 --> 00:15:41.386
We get a bit more space for that and to look into that.

00:15:41.386 --> 00:15:45.170
That&nbsp;gives us a bit more space to also 
look into social documentation.

00:15:48.639 --> 00:15:50.853
Documentation of social aspects.

00:15:50.853 --> 00:15:54.057
How do you document community building?

00:15:54.057 --> 00:15:58.805
How do you document feedback loops and the creating of&nbsp;trust?

00:16:00.471 --> 00:16:09.064
It's quite challenging, because basically you cannot really make a drawing 
or a blueprint of&nbsp;documenting conflict management.

00:16:09.064 --> 00:16:16.747
So, we are in this whole process of figuring out 
on how to make this&nbsp;long-term social documentation.

00:16:16.747 --> 00:16:22.668
And, basically at the foundation of it,
how to live&nbsp;a resilient life and to live in a sustainable way.

00:16:27.306 --> 00:16:31.691
- In our building project, we try to reuse&nbsp;as much materials as we can.

00:16:31.691 --> 00:16:35.325
And, we'll use as much recycled materials as possible.

00:16:35.325 --> 00:16:38.656
But, as we move forward, many&nbsp;questions pop up.

00:16:38.656 --> 00:16:41.911
These are questions that haven't got an easy answer.

00:16:42.411 --> 00:16:46.066
For example, this isolation is&nbsp;recycled textile.

00:16:46.066 --> 00:16:51.493
But, this needs always a new layer of plastic to make it watertight.

00:16:51.493 --> 00:16:56.388
This, for example, is cork, which is like a new material and it's natural.

00:16:56.388 --> 00:17:00.787
And, it doesn't need the use of&nbsp;this new plastic foil on the building.

00:17:01.304 --> 00:17:05.557
Another question is to bring non-natural 
materials to&nbsp;a natural environment.

00:17:05.557 --> 00:17:10.034
Which, when working with them, like cutting or moving around...

00:17:10.034 --> 00:17:16.002
It always&nbsp;releases some particles of plastic
 or different non-natural materials.

00:17:16.732 --> 00:17:18.642
So, this also rises the&nbsp;question like:

00:17:18.642 --> 00:17:23.074
''Is it nice to bring this kind of materials 
to this kind of environment?''

00:17:23.743 --> 00:17:31.589
So, this&nbsp;is our pig trailer, and these colors rise the question
 also of the recycled and reused look.

00:17:31.589 --> 00:17:34.691
Some&nbsp;people like it more, and some people like it less.

00:17:35.299 --> 00:17:38.436
I think it's quite funny anyway!

00:17:38.436 --> 00:17:42.203
But, this always comes&nbsp;back to that you need to reuse materials.

00:17:42.203 --> 00:17:46.852
You try to find the materials that accomplish the technical&nbsp;mission.

00:17:46.852 --> 00:17:51.299
But, maybe then you cannot have control on how it looks like...

00:17:51.816 --> 00:18:00.000
Even more, in this natural&nbsp;environment, 
introducing these looks can be a bit rough.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:04.368
- Okay! Those were our current challenges&nbsp;so far!

00:18:04.368 --> 00:18:05.755
Now, we're going for the Q&amp;A.

00:18:05.755 --> 00:18:08.133
This time, it's going to be a bit different.

00:18:08.133 --> 00:18:12.279
Because, it's going&nbsp;to be me walking around
 Project Kamp with this spoon microphone.

00:18:12.279 --> 00:18:13.956
Asking questions to the people!

00:18:13.956 --> 00:18:19.460
And,&nbsp;then also, Adrian and Julie had to leave,
 but they are going to answer remotely.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:23.099
So, also&nbsp;that's something we want to try and integrate.

00:18:23.599 --> 00:18:24.552
Yeah! Let's see how it goes!

00:18:25.471 --> 00:18:30.722
Have you considered&nbsp;selling the mechanical parts of the yellow van, 
now that it's no longer a mobile vehicle?

00:18:32.536 --> 00:18:33.493
- I didn't&nbsp;consider it...

00:18:33.493 --> 00:18:35.173
They're in there and we don't use them!

00:18:35.173 --> 00:18:37.584
So, if you want to get them, you can come and take&nbsp;them out.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.641
- Okay, a question for Adrian!

00:18:39.641 --> 00:18:42.838
Why have you put the pig trailer so far away from the Kamp?

00:18:42.838 --> 00:18:47.477
- So,&nbsp;for now, we moved it mainly to have less noise in Basekamp.

00:18:47.477 --> 00:18:52.852
And, also, a nicer place to record and to&nbsp;make the projects.

00:18:53.308 --> 00:18:59.085
In the future, we probably want to move away a bit more
from the workspace to the&nbsp;living area.

00:18:59.085 --> 00:19:00.578
There's many reasons for it!

00:19:00.943 --> 00:19:06.906
But, so far, I cannot say way more,
because we still don't&nbsp;have a proper plan for it...

00:19:06.906 --> 00:19:11.240
- Do you crave any favorite fruits from your homeland,
 and how do you deal with it?

00:19:11.675 --> 00:19:16.098
Yeah,&nbsp;so, at this time of the year, I really miss 'pepernoten', 
which are these tiny little Dutch biscuits!

00:19:16.933 --> 00:19:20.560
I deal with it by getting the chefs to make it for us in the oven.

00:19:21.046 --> 00:19:24.571
- My mom's food and I can't deal with&nbsp;it.

00:19:24.571 --> 00:19:25.480
It's too much.

00:19:26.398 --> 00:19:30.565
- The only food I would crave would be French fries
 or Belgian fries, as we call&nbsp;them.

00:19:31.298 --> 00:19:34.044
If I want them, I can just make them,
because I work in the kitchen.

00:19:35.346 --> 00:19:36.289
- Asado!

00:19:36.289 --> 00:19:38.333
It's the Argentinian word for barbecue.

00:19:38.333 --> 00:19:43.020
And, actually, we did one a couple of weeks ago outside the Kamp.

00:19:43.354 --> 00:19:48.050
- Pa amb tomàquet, which is bread with&nbsp;tomato and a good olive oil!

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:52.676
Croissants and cabbage, and I never eat them.

00:19:52.676 --> 00:19:55.194
Then when I do, it will be amazing.

00:19:55.194 --> 00:19:57.909
- I do like a lot of Dutch cheese and chocolate sprinkles!

00:19:57.909 --> 00:20:02.839
So, every now and then, I have friends&nbsp;
or my family passing by to drop me some.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:09.377
- Roughly translated, it's called Tree Cake,
 because it has like&nbsp;layers like a tree.

00:20:11.726 --> 00:20:13.847
I don't deal with it, because I don't have it, so...

00:20:13.847 --> 00:20:15.803
I'm sad I guess.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:22.460
- I&nbsp;miss the salted butter, but there is olive oil, so it's okay!

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:26.729
Have you guys thought about making berms&nbsp;and swales
to hold more water in the ground?

00:20:27.185 --> 00:20:28.498
- Yeah! We thought about that!

00:20:29.495 --> 00:20:31.331
But, it wasn't really the&nbsp;time for it in the summer yet...

00:20:31.331 --> 00:20:32.844
We're actually starting it soon.

00:20:32.844 --> 00:20:35.455
So, in the recent updates,
 you would see us&nbsp;making quite a few of them.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.206
- Will you be planting more trees soon?

00:20:39.206 --> 00:20:40.285
Yes.

00:20:42.431 --> 00:20:43.480
- Hey Tincho!

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.798
A quick question:

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:48.212
When do you expect the tractor for Project Kamp?

00:20:49.056 --> 00:20:50.648
- We need two more ads!

00:20:52.555 --> 00:20:55.077
We're still thinking&nbsp;about if it's going 
to be a digger or a tractor.

00:20:55.077 --> 00:20:58.203
If you want more info, you can go to the research module.

00:20:58.203 --> 00:20:59.627
Link's in the&nbsp;description!

00:21:00.144 --> 00:21:02.223
- Oh, and also, there are many flies here, no?

00:21:02.223 --> 00:21:03.606
A lot!

00:21:04.275 --> 00:21:05.467
It's very annoying!

00:21:05.467 --> 00:21:06.520
- I'm going!

00:21:06.641 --> 00:21:08.737
What about the&nbsp;biogas, Timo?

00:21:08.737 --> 00:21:12.139
- Well, the biogas digestor has moved to its new place here.

00:21:12.139 --> 00:21:15.044
But, we're still waiting for&nbsp;the right gas to come out of it...

00:21:15.044 --> 00:21:17.129
You'll hear about it in a future update.

00:21:17.129 --> 00:21:18.301
- This one is for Julie!

00:21:18.727 --> 00:21:22.787
What is the average number of people 
involved in the Kamp on a daily basis on site?

00:21:22.787 --> 00:21:24.153
- Normally, around 25.

00:21:24.153 --> 00:21:26.360
Sometimes, when neighbors come, it's 27.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:29.149
When people are on holidays: 24...

00:21:29.149 --> 00:21:32.051
Now that we are not there, it's 22.

00:21:32.051 --> 00:21:34.595
So, it's always&nbsp;different!

00:21:34.595 --> 00:21:39.686
- Is there something that newcomers to the Kamp 
were surprised about or expecting differently?

00:21:40.325 --> 00:21:43.338
I think at least myself and I think other people too...

00:21:43.338 --> 00:21:45.860
Everything is way steeper like super steep!

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.966
And, in Basekamp, everything is closer together.

00:21:47.966 --> 00:21:50.596
In the video, it looks like more spread out 
but then it's&nbsp;closer together.

00:21:50.596 --> 00:21:52.802
But, mainly, everything's steep.

00:21:52.802 --> 00:21:54.639
- But, you are not a newcomer.

00:21:54.639 --> 00:21:58.963
No, I came long time ago, but&nbsp;still it's still steep for me...

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:01.586
- The size of this place!

00:22:02.377 --> 00:22:06.617
I thought it was smaller, but when you&nbsp;come here,
you can see that it's quite big.

00:22:06.617 --> 00:22:10.448
Every place is much closer than it looks like in the&nbsp;videos.

00:22:10.448 --> 00:22:13.351
And, also at night, it used to be very cold!

00:22:14.465 --> 00:22:19.512
- For me, I would say that my expectations&nbsp;
really match up with how it really is to live here.

00:22:19.512 --> 00:22:22.379
Cleaning the poop buckets is not as bad as&nbsp;it seems.

00:22:23.626 --> 00:22:26.128
- With the videos, there is always lot of things going on.

00:22:26.128 --> 00:22:30.284
At the Kamp, it's more chill and we can take&nbsp;a nap.

00:22:30.284 --> 00:22:32.450
It's better!

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:34.761
- How do you deal with the laundry of so many people?

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:36.856
It's actually not that&nbsp;bad!

00:22:37.356 --> 00:22:38.722
We have a beautiful washing machine.

00:22:38.722 --> 00:22:40.344
It's a Dutch washing machine over here.

00:22:40.344 --> 00:22:43.739
And sometimes, if it's been like really cloudy or a cloudy week...

00:22:43.739 --> 00:22:46.584
We have a bunch of people piling up 
as soon as the&nbsp;sun comes out!

00:22:46.584 --> 00:22:48.922
But, in the whole, it works pretty well.

00:22:48.922 --> 00:22:52.050
We share our loads when possible 
and hang them&nbsp;in the sun to dry.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:53.829
- What's your take on the mobility?

00:22:53.829 --> 00:22:58.841
Driving petrol cars has a huge impact 
on emissions&nbsp;and plastic particles.

00:22:58.841 --> 00:23:01.212
I almost never see bikes in your videos.

00:23:01.212 --> 00:23:03.857
Probably because the Kamp&nbsp;is too far away from everything...

00:23:04.435 --> 00:23:07.868
Well, we do use petrol cars, as you've seen.

00:23:07.868 --> 00:23:09.787
We renovated the pickup.

00:23:09.787 --> 00:23:11.531
And, we have the blue car, which is the caddy.

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:17.618
We are quite far away from many places, but...

00:23:17.618 --> 00:23:23.982
I live in a town nearby, and I take my bike
 almost every morning and cycle here, so...

00:23:23.982 --> 00:23:26.981
And,&nbsp;there are some other bikes inside there from the people.

00:23:26.981 --> 00:23:28.986
We take them every now and then, but the&nbsp;thing is...

00:23:28.986 --> 00:23:32.357
Normally, we don't move much away from the Kamp.

00:23:32.357 --> 00:23:33.864
Maybe in the weekends?

00:23:35.905 --> 00:23:38.664
It's&nbsp;more like one car driving with many people around.

00:23:38.664 --> 00:23:46.423
So, although we use cars, I think we are doing&nbsp;quite well, I would say.

00:23:46.423 --> 00:23:48.982
So, regarding solar electric production:

00:23:48.982 --> 00:23:52.372
What are your plans to prepare&nbsp;for a more steady occupation of the land?

00:23:52.372 --> 00:23:54.889
Do you change the orientation of your panels between seasons?

00:23:54.889 --> 00:23:58.901
Have&nbsp;you listed possible ways to 
use solar electricity surplus in summer?

00:23:58.901 --> 00:24:03.086
To produce energy intensive&nbsp;stuff 
that you will use later in the winter?

00:24:03.877 --> 00:24:07.859
- We are not thinking very much ahead yet.

00:24:07.859 --> 00:24:12.345
We cannot&nbsp;collect the electricity surplus
 that we have in summer for the winter.

00:24:12.345 --> 00:24:16.815
The only surplus that we collect so&nbsp;far is when it's very sunny.

00:24:16.815 --> 00:24:21.972
We connect the electric water boiler,
 and we keep hot water&nbsp;for later.

00:24:22.611 --> 00:24:25.512
So far, we never changed inclination, but we could!

00:24:25.512 --> 00:24:27.158
So, we are ready to put it up.

00:24:27.158 --> 00:24:29.517
- What you learned&nbsp;about communal living?

00:24:30.641 --> 00:24:33.334
There's always a shoulder to cry on.

00:24:33.851 --> 00:24:37.802
A little bit about communication,&nbsp;but not so much this far.

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:40.702
- What have you learned about communal living?

00:24:41.614 --> 00:24:43.251
It's great and there's always&nbsp;food!

00:24:43.981 --> 00:24:45.991
- Make sure to find your own time.

00:24:46.508 --> 00:24:50.395
What I've learned is that you really need to rely on other&nbsp;people.

00:24:51.186 --> 00:24:54.397
- That if somebody annoys you, it's you who's the problem.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:58.191
I have learned the importance of human&nbsp;touch.

00:24:59.498 --> 00:25:00.789
- It's a lot of fun!

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:07.155
That is hard work, but, bit by bit,
we are seeing the positive parts of communal living!

00:25:07.946 --> 00:25:14.167
- I don't really know, but it's nice to live all these days
 with lot of different people.

00:25:14.471 --> 00:25:16.013
So, you didn't&nbsp;get tired yet?

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:18.105
Hey!

00:25:19.666 --> 00:25:23.341
One question: what have you learn about communal living?

00:25:24.588 --> 00:25:26.655
- You need to find your own space.

00:25:27.324 --> 00:25:28.281
Like now?

00:25:28.281 --> 00:25:29.435
- Just like now.

00:25:29.435 --> 00:25:30.470
Sorry!

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:34.354
Have you guys considered a biogas plan
to help with the poop situation?

00:25:35.631 --> 00:25:38.807
- At the moment, our poop situation is 
kind of covered with composting.

00:25:45.388 --> 00:25:48.839
And, will you be adding animals to the Kamp?

00:25:48.961 --> 00:25:49.609
- I don't know!

00:25:49.609 --> 00:25:50.742
People talk about goats?

00:25:50.742 --> 00:25:51.334
I don't know...

00:25:51.334 --> 00:25:52.781
Does it make sense to get goats?

00:25:52.781 --> 00:25:53.895
- I&nbsp;like goats!

00:25:54.457 --> 00:25:55.919
What do you guys think? Should we get goats?

00:25:55.919 --> 00:25:57.295
- Yeah, should we?

00:25:57.295 --> 00:25:59.608
What is the big&nbsp;biggest time consuming thing in a week?

00:26:00.295 --> 00:26:04.526
- We move a lot of stuff around since 
we're working at the pig trailer and in the workspace.

00:26:04.526 --> 00:26:06.559
They're not in the same working spot.

00:26:09.105 --> 00:26:13.393
- Probably&nbsp;making everything from scratch,
because we don't have all of the sorts of milk.

00:26:13.996 --> 00:26:16.928
We&nbsp;stopped buying tofu and saitan.

00:26:16.928 --> 00:26:20.043
So, we have to make that ourselves also.

00:26:20.043 --> 00:26:22.738
Which is quite time&nbsp;consuming, yeah...

00:26:22.738 --> 00:26:25.584
For us, in General Help, I think is moving the mimosas.

00:26:25.584 --> 00:26:30.268
Cutting them is okay, but like&nbsp;dragging them
 making a pile and then dealing with the pile...

00:26:30.268 --> 00:26:32.734
That takes weeks and weeks.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:36.878
- Chester,&nbsp;what is the most time consuming thing in Project Kamp?

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:44.167
So, I think for the bread team the most
&nbsp;time consuming thing is getting this thing hot enough!

00:26:44.167 --> 00:26:45.537
It takes about 2 and a half hours!

00:26:46.115 --> 00:26:48.617
It's&nbsp;actually still warm from yesterday's baking, but yeah...

00:26:48.617 --> 00:26:49.779
That takes a lot of time.

00:26:49.779 --> 00:26:51.976
- 3D-modeling&nbsp;for sure.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:56.051
What about updating and posting videos on winter season?

00:26:56.051 --> 00:27:00.687
- Well, during the the winter,&nbsp;we just want to chill.

00:27:00.687 --> 00:27:03.076
It's been an intense season, so...

00:27:03.076 --> 00:27:08.216
But, anyway, we will probably post some updates in Patreon.

00:27:08.733 --> 00:27:13.644
- Could you give us some stats on how the electric 
and water systems&nbsp;are performing with more people in Kamp?

00:27:13.644 --> 00:27:18.352
So, about our stats, we don't have much water stats collected.

00:27:18.352 --> 00:27:20.799
We recently start a sensor there.

00:27:20.799 --> 00:27:24.737
But, we still don't have the proper stats to share.

00:27:24.737 --> 00:27:26.481
So,&nbsp;we don't know, and we didn't analyze it...

00:27:27.363 --> 00:27:34.706
About the solar stats, here you can see
 the September 2022 stats with&nbsp;between 10 and 15 people.

00:27:34.706 --> 00:27:36.739
The battery was always high!

00:27:36.739 --> 00:27:39.783
It's the sunniest time of the year, so it was good.

00:27:40.543 --> 00:27:45.404
Here you have stats from 2023,
with around 25 people in the Kamp.

00:27:45.404 --> 00:27:48.016
The same amount of panels
 and the same amount of&nbsp;batteries.

00:27:48.016 --> 00:27:50.816
But, the battery, as you can see, is way lower.

